{% figura src="/images/vignettes/issa-30.png" alt="Line drawing of a bedroom plan with a bed, shelving and a figure in a gaming chair at a desk with monitors" /%}

*Hyperstreaming: Live broadcasting with explosive Real-Time Chatting.*

The Bedroom. Livestreaming as a concept began in the early 1990s with experimental tech demos, and in 2011, the twitch platform was founded. Despite being created for the livestreaming of gaming content, it functions almost as a digital, decentralized, pop-up club venue, allowing DJs to stream raw, unedited practice sessions directly from their bedrooms or studio setups. Twitch, in particular, allows viewers to chat and react to one another and the content, in real time. This chat feature moves at hyperspeed, and streamers often hint at or mention comments or donations that their viewers have made. If the content creator gets a notification in real time, It’s like a digital hangout; there are the elements of connection and conversation, action and reaction, although with some distance.

Not every person on twitch is an artist. Commentators, Gamers, Musicians, and Internet personalities coexist on this platform, and usually we get a semi-staged view of their bedrooms. Maybe the living room is a shared space; they might live with their parents, or roommates. Are they really like this in real life? Or is the room a backdrop for their gaming channel? Usually, when someone is invited inside someone’s home, or if guests are present, the bedroom is avoided. Here, the private space of the bedroom is amplified, with shelving systems of toys, records, and neon signs, located behind the silhouette of a gaming chair. Some have two monitors, some have three. Those that are at an advanced level, have one, hugely curved monitor. Bet you didn’t see that coming. Distasteful architectural decisions are made, almost always when it comes to the lighting of the space. Neon RGB colored strips, or even worse, overwhelming wall mounted LED signage…. The interface itself is overwhelming; an overwhelming stream of data all at once.

Today, artists utilize short-form content to capture quick, high-energy moments, in real time. Seamless track transitions, genre switch-ups, or crowd reactions at gigs are things we can commonly see on our phones and on social media. There are two extremes to this: creators who stream for hours every day, and the viewers who listen for hours every day, the stream becoming ambient sound to the mundane tasks viewers may be doing at home. And, the content creators who recycle and shorten these streams, converting them into thirty-second digestible clips for those who have a lesser attention span. This kind of short-form recycled content has become an entire economy; something that people can use as a side-hustle.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/issa-31.jpg" alt="Twitch directory with rows of live stream thumbnails in the Just Chatting and IRL categories" ancho="completo" /%}

**Contents: Webcam, Large Desk, Two/Three-Part Monitor System, Headphones, Shelf of Stuff, Gaming Chair.**
